<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greiz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Greiz is a town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-elster">White Elster</a> in the Vogtland. From 1564 it was the seat of the Elder Line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-reuss">House of Reuss</a>, whose princes ruled from its Upper and Lower Castles and summer palace until 1918. In the 19th century it became a centre of woollen textile manufacturing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greiz.jpg" alt="Greiz" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steffen Löwe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greiz_2013-05_0725_Blick_vom_Oberen_Schloss.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Greiz lies in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, 100 kilometres east of the state capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-elster">White Elster</a> river. It stands at the confluence of the White Elster and its tributary the Göltzsch, a position that helped the town grow fast. The place name, originally Grouts, is of Slavic origin and means gord, a fortified settlement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first documented mention of the settlement dates from 1209. From the 12th century it was governed by advocati, or Vögte, and in 1236 it came into the possession of Gera. It was recognised as a town in the 13th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-reuss">House of Reuss</a>, a ruling dynasty whose male members were all named Heinrich, later built two castles there, the Upper Castle and the Lower Castle, both by noted architects. Greiz became the capital of the Principality of Reuss-Greiz, which lasted until 1918. Fire destroyed the town entirely in 1494 and almost totally in 1802.</p><p>Between 1934 and 1943, 809 people were forcibly sterilised by the Nazis in the district hospital, and a local old people's home and care home sent 122 sick people to the Aktion T4 euthanasia programme. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> prisoners of war and people from occupied countries worked as forced labourers at Sorgwald and elsewhere, and at least 102 died; there is a memorial in the Old Cemetery. A monument honours the officer Kurt von Westernhagen, shot by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> in April 1945 for refusing to blow up the bridge and defend the town. Three of the five bridges were destroyed, but the town was not otherwise much damaged. Ulf Merbold, the first West German astronaut, was born there in 1941.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Greiz is the capital of the district of Greiz and has about 20,400 inhabitants. It has a large park, classified as an English garden, in its centre, the Fürstlich Greizer Park, and streets of Jugendstil houses. Its economy includes mechanical engineering, plastics, medical technology, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> and breweries. Like many towns in the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> it has lost population.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Greiz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Principality of Reuss Elder Line at <b>Greiz</b> and the Principality of Reuss Younger Line at Gera both became free states in November 1918 after their princes abdicated.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-state-of-reuss">People's State of Reuss</a></li><li><blockquote>In the division of the Reuss lands in 1564, the Elder Line received <b>Greiz</b> and the surrounding part of the Vogtland.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-reuss-greiz">Principality of Reuss-Greiz</a></li></ul></section>
